  "worked_solution": "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{3}\u2061x({sec}^{3}\u2061x-\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x) & ={cos}^{3}\u2061x\\cdot {sec}^{3}\u2061x-{cos}^{3}\u2061x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}$ to further simplify the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{3}\u2061x\\cdot {sec}^{3}\u2061x-{cos}^{3}\u2061x\\cdot \\operatorname{sec}\u2061x & ={cos}^{3}\u2061x\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x})}^{3}-{cos}^{3}\u2061x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}\u2061x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{3}\u2061x}-{cos}^{3}\u2061x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{3}\u2061x}{{cos}^{3}\u2061x}-\\frac{{cos}^{3}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{3}\u2061x}}{\\cancel{{cos}^{3}\u2061x}}-\\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061x\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x}} \\\\  & =1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can use the identity ${sin}^{2}\u2061x=1-{cos}^{2}\u2061x$ to rewrite the expression on the right-hand side.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{3}\u2061x({sec}^{3}\u2061x-\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x)={sin}^{2}\u2061x\n$$",
